r prepares the CA, issuance profile and DMS."],[653,"The alerts API reference documents the full `http_client` schema."],[235,"The AMQP client loads certificates at startup, so restart the consuming pods after updating either Secret. Changing any `amqp` value rolls the consuming pods through the `checksum/config` annotation, but a change to Secret content alone does not trigger a rollout."],[1645,"The API reference contains contracts for automating operations. To investigate a failure, start in the affected section: PKI, EST or Deployment."],[1857,"The application permits that identity to perform the action."],[457,"the applied change and how to revert it;"],[1479,"The approved root for Lamassu HTTPS in `est-trust.pem`, obtained through an independent channel. It can differ from the device CA; prepare networking/TLS in Deployment."],[1578,"The associated certificate matches the one you verified in PKI."],[1580,"The associated issuance appears with its serial number and dates."],[694,"The audit body includes:"],[929,"The authorization response must be JSON of at most 1 MiB; a larger body fails the call."],[549,"The available modes are:"],[566,"The available operators are `eq`, `neq`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`, `in` and `like`. Use comparisons consistent with the column type: `like` for text, ordering comparisons for numbers or dates and `in` when there is a collection of accepted values."],[1252,"The AWS IoT Core connector is an independent Go process, a binary or container separate from Lamassu's core. In a monolithic deployment it is compiled by default (build tag `!noaws`) and activated with `aws_iot_manager.enabled: true` in the configuration file. In distributed deployments, it runs as its own container with access to Lamassu's CA, DMS Manager and Device Manager services over HTTP."],[1452,"The AWS IoT Core guide explains how to use the identity in that destination. Provisioning in Lamassu and provisioning in the destination are separate decisions; review the conditions of the chosen mode before expecting state synchronization."],[10,"The AWS Marketplace offering reduces initial setup to an EC2 instance inside your account. You keep control of the network, storage and operation of the machine."],[2099,"The backend also provides adapters for **Key Vault Keys** and **Key Vault Secrets**. The former delegates signing to the key service; the latter stores private material as secrets and signs using that material. These are different custody models. Confirm which adapter is enabled in your installation and test the required operations."],[701,"The backend applies auditing to mutations of:"],[815,"The backend saves **Decommissioned** and attempts to revoke the active version’s certificate with `CessationOfOperation` if the slot was not expired or revoked. It does not traverse all history. Deferred revocation can fail after retirement is saved; check the result by serial."],[886,"The blocks are independent:"],[1022,"The body is **MIME multipart/mixed**, with the boundary specified in `Content-Type`. It contains two parts:"],[1967,"The CA hierarchy defines which authorities can sign. A root CA establishes the anchor of trust; intermediate CAs separate operating domains and avoid using the root for every issuance."],[2565,"The CA migration adds a UNIQUE constraint on `ca_certificates.id` and aborts if duplicate CA IDs exist. Imports already reject duplicates with `409`, so new ones cannot normally appear."],[1366,"The CA must be registered in AWS in Primary Account mode (SNI-only mode does not support JITP)."],[2287,"The CA name becomes its `Common Name`. Keep the CA usages needed to sign certificates and CRLs."],[2281,"The CA private key is used through the engine. In this path, you will download only the authority's public certificate."],[2337,"The CA rejects issuance"],[744,"The CA service issues certificates and maintains the relationship with its authority. The DMS applies the enrollment rules and exposes the EST flows so a device can request or renew an identity."],[1927,"The CA signs the CSR. The issuance profile determines the validity, uses, subject, allowed extensions and cryptographic constraints."],[2055,"The CA view lets you inspect and download the available list. Compare version, issuer and timestamps with the copy downloaded from the public endpoint. Diagnose differences using PKI troubleshooting."],[1742,"The CA's internal identifier is generated automatically and cannot be changed."],[1773,"The CA’s VA role is kept but can no longer be edited (see CRL), so adjust its validity, interval and signer beforehand. The CA’s key is released, but the KMS keeps it while other certificates still reference it."],[1166,"The certificate and key do not form a matching pair. Enter both values from the same pair; the check runs when the DMS is saved and again on every webhook call."],[323,"The certificate contains the DNS name or IP used by the client."],[1928,"The certificate enters the `ACTIVE` state. Lamassu monitors its `Not After` date; the cryptographic monitoring job marks certificates that have expired as `EXPIRED`."],[2245,"The certificate's purpose, such as authenticating a client through mTLS."],[2030,"The certificates of all of them point to `/crl/{ski}`, but the published list has the first CA as issuer and only includes its revocations. A verifier compares the CRL issuer with the issuer of the certificate it validates (RFC 5280, §6.3.3) and rejects the list if they differ. Depending on its policy, the certificate is blocked (*hard-fail*) or accepted even though it is revoked (*soft-fail*). Revocations of the secondary CAs do not appear in any list."],[2385,"The chain validates but the connection fails"],[452,"The chart can enable OpenTelemetry instrumentation. Traces are exported over OTLP HTTP and logs can be directed to a compatible endpoint, such as VictoriaLogs. Use a correlation identifier and the same time window to join Gateway, authz, service and dependency."],[371,"The chart creates Lamassu's `Gateway` and routes. It does not install the Envoy Gateway controller or make the generated `LoadBalancer` Service reachable from your network."],[373,"The chart creates one shared `Gateway` per Helm release. All chart-managed `HTTPRoute` resources attach to it. Envoy Gateway provisions the proxy data plane and a Kubernetes `LoadBalancer` Service for that Gateway."],[219,"The chart defaults to a `GatewayClass` named `eg`. Change `gateway.className` if your platform team uses another name."],[221,"The chart has no Helm dependencies that install PostgreSQL, RabbitMQ, an OIDC provider, Envoy Gateway or cert-manager."],[586,"The chart includes a bootstrap principal that looks for the OIDC role `pki-admin` in `realm_access.roles`. It grants it `SUPER ADMIN` and, when Job Manager is enabled, the administrative policy of its NBI API."],[233,"The chart mounts configured Secrets read-only into every AMQP-consuming workload: the CA certificate under `/etc/lamassu/amqp/ca` and the client certificate and key under `/etc/lamassu/amqp/client`. The mounted file names are always `ca.crt`, `tls.crt` and `tls.key` — the `certificateKey` and `privateKeyKey` values rename only the Secret data keys, never the on-disk paths. The client private key is mounted with restrictive permissions (`defaultMode: 0440`; the CA volume uses `0444`), which works because the chart adds `fsGroup` to the pod security context when `amqp.client.secretName` is set — it uses the container `runAsGroup` (default `65532`) when `fsGroup` is not already set."],[410,"The chart's services expose startup, readiness and liveness probes over /health. A pod can be running but receive no traffic if the readiness probe fails."],[1045,"The client requests an identity for its `Device ID` through EST."],[1021,"The code supports RSA and ECDSA, and the `bits` value of the DMS key setting means something different for each. For ECDSA it selects the curve: 224, 256, 384 or 521 map to P-224, P-256, P-384 and P-521, and an unrecognized size falls back to P-256, so explicitly select a compatible size and check the resulting key. For RSA it is used directly as the modulus size; the console offers 2048, 3072 and 4096 bits. Do not reuse an enrolled ID to test this operation without reviewing replacement."],[2331,"The command does not query OCSP or CRL. For revocation checks, continue with Certificate validation. See the openssl-verify reference for its options."],[456,"the commands or queries used;"],[253,"The complete values reference is maintained in `charts/lamassu/VALUES.md` in the Helm repository."],[454,"the confirmed cause;"],[1387,"The connector can write to the AWS IoT Core Device Shadow to send action signals to the device firmware. This feature is enabled per DMS with `shadow_config.enable: true`."],[1394,"The connector creates and attaches the policies configured in the DMS. A typical policy for a device using MQTT and the Device Shadow has this structure:"],[1356,"The connector creates the template, named after the DMS ID, only once. Each later edit of the DMS that keeps the connector key tries to create it again and fails, because the connector does not update existing templates. Later changes to `jitp_config.provisioning_role_arn`, `jitp_config.enable_template` or to the groups and policies the template references must be applied by hand in AWS IoT Core."],[1253,"The connector does not poll Lamassu's services periodically. Instead, it **subscribes to Lamassu's internal event bus** (AMQP or AWS SQS/SNS, configurable) and reacts to the events it cares about: CA creation or update, DMS creation or update, device identity binding, certificate status change, certificate metadata update and device metadata update. This event-driven model ensures synchronization with AWS is nearly immediate and produces no polling load."],[1340,"The connector internally generates an ephemeral RSA-2048 key pair and builds a CSR whose Common Name is exactly that registration code. It then calls Lamassu's signing service so the registered CA issues a real X.509 verification certificate signing that CSR. The ephemeral private key is used only for this purpose and is neither stored nor sent to AWS."],[1370,"The connector listens for the `EventUpdateCertificateStatusKey` event and, when a certificate holding the connector's metadata key changes status in Lamassu, replicates that change in AWS IoT Core. The following table shows the status mapping:"],[1389,"The connector writes to the `desired.identity_actions` field of the shadow document. Each entry contains a Unix timestamp in milliseconds. The device firmware must read this field and act accordingly."],[742,"The console and the APIs manage authorities, certificates, DMSs, devices, alerts and configuration. Domain events allow connectors and automations to react to changes without continuously polling the platform."],[2257,"the consumer applies revocation checks and authorization."],[1970,"The consumer keeps its own trust store. EST `/cacerts` can distribute the service TLS certificate, the enrollment CA and selected managed CAs. Initial trust for authenticating that service must come from an already trusted channel. Download and inspect the contents: do not assume they form a complete chain or install every entry as a root."],[729,"the consumer persists it exactly once;"],[1047,"The consumer validates and authorizes the identity; the client schedules renewal and recovery."],[1625,"The consumer validates the identity and applies its access permissions."],[1029,"The controller also adds `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64` to the outer header, although the outer body is MIME text. &#x2A;*Do not Base64-decode the entire body.** Read the actual boundary and decode each part according to its headers."],[616,"The credential could be processed but did not produce a positive authorization. Review in this order:"],[1760,"The CRL linked to the authority."],[1804,"The cryptographic enforcement can:"],[2083,"The Cryptographic Key Management Service (KMS) of Lamassu IoT centralizes the generation, import, custody and use of the platform's keys. This is where you manage the keys that support the CAs and the rest of the signing operations that depend on the PKI."],[505,"The decision is made **before forwarding the request to the service**:"],[525,"The decision is made with the domain operation context:"],[976,"The default content is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7. It is not a direct PEM file."],[1014,"The default response has `Content-Type: application/pkcs7-mime; smime-type=certs-only` and `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64`. The body is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7 with lines of up to 76 characters. It includes the certificates selected by the DMS, not necessarily every ancestor."],[1190,"The default response is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7; PEM through `Accept: application/x-pem-file` is a Lamassu extension. ServerKeyGen returns multipart and requires handling each part separately. See the EST reference."],[1017,"The default response uses the PKCS#7/Base64 format described for cacerts, but contains the issued certificate; do not assume it includes its entire chain. `Accept: application/x-pem-file` returns that certificate in PEM. The client retains the key that signed the CSR."],[1624,"The device client requests a certificate and installs it with the matching key."],[759,"The device generates a key and requests a certificate through EST, or an operator assigns it an identity."],[1953,"The device history relates its previous and current identities. For administrative changes and mutation errors, see Audit logs."],[1974,"The device installs the certificate and the chain it needs."],[1368,"The device must present both its certificate and the issuing CA's certificate in the TLS handshake with AWS IoT Core."],[1971,"The device proves it can enroll according to the DMS policy."],[1936,"The device view may add operational states such as missing identity, pending renewal, expiring soon